\[
\boxed{\text{The silicon atom is only stereogenic because the two morpholino rings are locked in a twisted, non‑planar arrangement that creates enantiomeric conformers. In solution these rings can rotate (or the Si centre can undergo low‑energy inversion), allowing rapid interconversion between the two mirror‑image conformations. This conformational interconversion occurs faster than the NMR timescale, so the chiral environments are averaged and the populations of the two enantiomers become equal. Consequently, despite a stereogenic Si centre, the molecule is racemic in solution because its chirality is conformational, not configurationally fixed.}}
\]

\[
\boxed{\text{Ha (O–CH}_2\text{ pointing toward the phenyl ring)} \;>\; 
       \text{Hb (O–CH}_2\text{ pointing away from the phenyl ring)} \;>\; 
       \text{Hc (N–CH}_2\text{ pointing toward the phenyl ring)} \;>\; 
       \text{Hd (N–CH}_2\text{ pointing away from the phenyl ring)}}
\]

**Justification (qualitative).**  
The phenyl group generates a strong aromatic ring‑current anisotropy: protons lying in the plane of the ring (the deshielding region) appear down‑field, whereas protons situated above or below the plane (the shielding cone) appear up‑field. In the twisted conformation of Cl–Si(Ph)(Mor)₂, one proton of the O‑CH₂ group (Ha) points into the aromatic deshielding zone and is also close to the Si–Cl bond, whose own anisotropy adds further deshielding. Consequently Ha is the most down‑field signal. The second O‑CH₂ proton (Hb) points away from the phenyl plane but remains near the Si–Cl bond and is still deshielded by the electronegative oxygen, giving the next down‑field resonance.  

The N‑CH₂ protons are less deshielded because nitrogen is less electronegative than oxygen. Of these, the proton that points toward the phenyl ring (Hc) lies near the aromatic plane and experiences modest deshielding, placing it at an intermediate chemical shift. The opposite N‑CH₂ proton (Hd) points into the shielding cone of the phenyl ring and is farthest from the Si–Cl deshielding region, so it experiences the greatest shielding and appears most up‑field.  

Thus the spatial orientation of each CH₂ proton with respect to the aromatic anisotropy and the Si–Cl bond, together with the differing electronegativities of O and N, rationalises the observed order Ha > Hb > Hc > Hd.

