For a two‑site exchange (A ↔ B) with equal populations the Bloch–McConnell treatment gives, in the slow‑to‑intermediate regime, the coalescence condition

\[
k_{c}= \frac{\pi\,\Delta\nu}{\sqrt{2}}\;,
\qquad 
\Delta\nu = \nu_{0}\,\Delta\delta \;(\text{Hz}) .
\tag{1}
\]

(If the intrinsic linewidth \(\Delta\nu_{1/2}\) is not negligible the more general form is  

\[
k_{c}= \frac{\pi}{\sqrt{2}}\,
\sqrt{\Delta\nu^{2}+\Delta\nu_{1/2}^{2}}\; .
\tag{1'}
\])

The temperature dependence of the exchange rate is described by transition‑state theory (Eyring equation)

\[
k_{c}= \frac{k_{\!B}\,T_{c}}{h}\;
\exp\!\Big(-\frac{\Delta G^{\ddagger}}{R\,T_{c}}\Big) .
\tag{2}
\]

Combining (1) (or (1′)) with (2) gives the required relation between the coalescence temperature \(T_{c}\) and the exchange rate constant at coalescence:

\[
\boxed{\;
\frac{\pi\,\nu_{0}\,\Delta\delta}{\sqrt{2}}
\;=\;
\frac{k_{\!B}\,T_{c}}{h}\,
\exp\!\Big(-\frac{\Delta G^{\ddagger}}{R\,T_{c}}\Big)
\;}
\qquad\text{(or with \(\Delta\nu_{1/2}\):}
\boxed{\;
\frac{\pi}{\sqrt{2}}\sqrt{(\nu_{0}\Delta\delta)^{2}+(\Delta\nu_{1/2})^{2}}
=
\frac{k_{\!B}\,T_{c}}{h}\,
\exp\!\Big(-\frac{\Delta G^{\ddagger}}{R\,T_{c}}\Big)
\;}
\]

Thus the coalescence temperature is linked to the exchange rate constant through the Eyring expression, with the rate constant at coalescence given by the NMR‑derived value \(\pi\Delta\nu/\sqrt{2}\).

