| 1b – PA | 10.0 | –8.0 |

**Kinetic control** – the product that forms fastest is the one with the lower activation barrier.  

\[
\frac{k_{\text{PA}}}{k_{\text{FA}}}=e^{-(\Delta G^{\ddagger}_{\text{PA}}-\Delta G^{\ddagger}_{\text{FA}})/RT}
= e^{-(10.0-12.5)/(0.001987\times298)}
= e^{4.22}\approx 6.8\times10^{1}
\]

Thus PA forms ≈ 70 times faster than FA; the kinetically favored monoadduct is **PA**.

**Thermodynamic control** – the more stable product is the one with the more negative reaction free energy.  

\[
\frac{K_{\text{PA}}}{K_{\text{FA}}}=e^{-(\Delta G_{\text{PA}}-\Delta G_{\text{FA}})/RT}
= e^{-(-8.0+5.0)/(0.001987\times298)}
= e^{5.06}\approx 1.6\times10^{2}
\]

PA is ≈ 160 times more favored at equilibrium than FA; the thermodynamically preferred monoadduct is also **PA**.

\[
\boxed{\text{PA (pyrone‑side adduct) is preferred under both kinetic and thermodynamic control.}}
