\text{(a)}&\ \text{ν(CO) shifts to lower wavenumber (red‑shift) relative to the Ru}_3\text{(CO)}_{10}\text{ cluster}.\\[4pt]
\text{(b)}&\ \text{The bridging‑hydride dihydride exhibits a higher ν(CO) (blue‑shift) compared with the chelate‑terminal dihydride isomer.}
\end{aligned}
}
\]

**Justification**

*Oxidative‑addition product (a):*  
The reaction replaces two π‑accepting CO ligands with a strongly σ‑donating bridging hydride and a bridging thiolate. Both new ligands increase the electron density at the Ru atoms, while the loss of CO (a strong π‑acceptor) further raises metal electron density. Greater metal→CO back‑bonding weakens the C–O bond, lowering the ν(CO) frequencies (red‑shift).

*Bridging‑ vs terminal‑hydride dihydrides (b):*  
A terminal hydride donates its electron pair to a single metal centre, providing a strong σ‑donor effect that markedly raises the electron density at that Ru. This enhances back‑bonding to its CO ligands, giving lower ν(CO) values. In a bridging hydride the H atom shares its donation between two metals, so each metal receives less electron density than from a terminal hydride. Consequently the CO ligands experience weaker back‑bonding and their ν(CO) stretches appear at higher wavenumber (blue‑shift) relative to the chelate‑terminal dihydride.

